Alembic database migrations
===========================

Single shared schema across all apps. `alembic/env.py` reads DATABASE_URL from
the environment and imports every app's models onto the one shared Base, so a
single migration tree covers strava, wakatime and projects.

Everyday usage (via the Makefile, which runs alembic inside an app container):

    make migrate                     # apply all pending migrations (upgrade head)
    make migration name="add x"      # autogenerate a migration from model changes
    make migrate-down                # roll back the most recent migration

`make dev-up` runs `make migrate` automatically, so a fresh local stack ends up
fully schema'd.

Adopting Alembic on a database that ALREADY has the tables
----------------------------------------------------------
The apps used to build tables with `Base.metadata.create_all()` at import. An
existing database (e.g. production) therefore already has every table but no
`alembic_version` row. Running `upgrade head` there would try to re-create the
tables and fail. Adopt Alembic ONCE with:

    make db-stamp                    # local dev DB
    # production: run `alembic stamp head` once in the app container/host

After stamping, all future deploys simply run `alembic upgrade head`.

Authoring new migrations
------------------------
1. Change the SQLAlchemy models under apps/<service>/models.py.
2. `make migration name="describe change"`.
3. Review the generated file in alembic/versions/ (autogenerate is a starting
   point, not gospel — check data migrations, server defaults, enum changes).
4. `make migrate` to apply locally; commit the version file.
